        </div>
        <div class="utility-toast" id="utility-toast" hidden>Link copied to clipboard</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="prose container">
    <p class="lead-intro">
      Every photo captured on modern smartphones and digital cameras embeds extensive personal information inside hidden file headers. Standard JPEG and PNG files routinely store precise GPS coordinates marking your home or workplace, exact shutter timestamps, camera lens serial numbers, and complete editing histories from software like Photoshop and Lightroom. When shared on social media, public forums, or listing marketplaces, this hidden data exposes your physical location and device fingerprint. RemoveSynth provides a free, client-side photo metadata remover that strips EXIF, IPTC, and XMP metadata directly inside your browser memory. With zero server uploads, no account registration, and zero loss in visual resolution, creators can sanitize up to 30 images simultaneously with complete privacy.
    </p>

    <h2>How does photo metadata removal work? Binary segment slicing formula</h2>
    <p>
      In standard digital image containers like JPEG (JFIF), image data is divided into structured binary marker segments beginning with <code>0xFF</code>. The primary segments include:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>0xFFD8</code>: Start of Image (SOI).</li>
      <li><code>0xFFE1</code>: APP1 segment containing EXIF (Exchangeable Image File Format) and XMP metadata.</li>
      <li><code>0xFFED</code>: APP13 segment containing IPTC records.</li>
      <li><code>0xFFDB</code> / <code>0xFFC4</code>: Quantization and Huffman coding tables.</li>
      <li><code>0xFFDA</code>: Start of Scan (SOS) followed by the entropy-coded image bitstream.</li>
      <li><code>0xFFD9</code>: End of Image (EOI).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Traditional web converters decode the image into uncompressed pixels and re-compress it into a new JPEG, causing generational quality loss, color banding, and blurred text. RemoveSynth operates entirely at the binary container layer:
    </p>
    <div class="math-box">
      <code>File_out = SOI + &sum; Marker_clean + SOS_bitstream + EOI</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Worked Example with Real Numbers:</strong> Consider a 4.2 MB photo taken with a mirrorless digital camera:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Total Original File Size: <code>4,242,500 bytes</code></li>
      <li>APP1 EXIF Segment (GPS, lens serial, exposure): <code>28,450 bytes</code></li>
      <li>APP1 XMP Segment (Adobe DocumentAncestors edit history): <code>54,200 bytes</code></li>
      <li>Embedded JPEG Thumbnail preview: <code>18,100 bytes</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      RemoveSynth locates the <code>0xFFE1</code> and <code>0xFFED</code> markers, calculates their segment length headers, and splices the file memory buffer so the output retains the <code>4,141,750 bytes</code> of pristine pixel scan data while completely eliminating the <code>100,750 bytes</code> of private tracking metadata. The result is 100 percent bit-for-bit identical visual clarity with zero image re-compression.
    </p>

    <h2>Step-by-step guide to removing EXIF and GPS metadata online</h2>
    <div class="steps-grid">
      <div class="step-card">
        <h3>Step 1: Select your photos</h3>
        <p>
          Drag and drop up to 30 JPG, PNG, or WebP files into the uploader above. The tool handles single pictures or large photo batches smoothly in RAM.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-card">
        <h3>Step 2: In-browser binary metadata stripping</h3>
        <p>
          RemoveSynth walks the binary byte tree, locating and removing GPS coordinates, camera serial numbers, and edit histories without touching pixel data.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-card">
        <h3>Step 3: Review sanitized file summary</h3>
        <p>
          Check the real-time summary table to verify stripped metadata tags, byte savings, and image orientation preservation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-card">
        <h3>Step 4: Download cleaned images</h3>
        <p>
          Download your sanitized photos individually or export all files as a single ZIP archive, completely safe for public sharing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Binary JPEG container segment layout before and after cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      The diagram below illustrates how RemoveSynth cleanly bypasses metadata segments without modifying the underlying image quantization, Huffman tables, or scan data.
    </p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 760 320" class="chart-svg" role="img" aria-label="Binary JPEG container segment structure showing stripped APP1 EXIF and XMP headers">
        <!-- Original File Container Track -->
        <text x="80" y="45" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.7)" font-size="12" font-weight="600">Original Photo Container (With Private Metadata):</text>
